  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A warming trend is expected through Thursday \nwith mostly dry conditions.\n\nLatest surface analysis shows 1026 mb high pressure offshore of\nthe lower Mid-Atlantic coast, ridging southwest across the Deep\nSouth. Aloft, a broad upper-level ridge remains anchored across\nthe southern third of the CONUS. To the north, low pressure \ncontinues to track across northern Ontario toward Quebec, \npushing an attendant surface cold front south-southwest across \nthe Upper Great Lakes and upper Midwest into the mid- Missouri \nValley.\n\nMainly sunny early this afternoon with only scattered fair-\nweather cumulus. 18Z temperatures are largely in the low to mid \n80s across the region, w/ breezy south-southwest winds gusting\nto 15-20 kt. As the surface high drifts farther offshore \ntonight, light but steady return flow will keep temperatures \nslightly milder but still comfortable. Look for overnight lows \ndropping into the mid to upper 60s inland, low 70s along the \ncoast, under a mainly clear sky.\n\nThe northern upper trough and associated surface low will \nslowly shift east-southeast tonight and Thursday, gradually \nsuppressing the ridge. The cold front approaches from the \nnorthwest tomorrow, slowing as the boundary aligns parallel to \nthe mid- and upper-level quasi-zonal flow. Compressional heating\nahead of the front will yield the warmest day of the period on \nThursday, with highs reaching the low-to-mid 90s inland from the\nimmediate coast. Recent 12Z guidance trends slightly higher for\nisolated to widely scattered convection entering western areas \nlate Thursday afternoon, spreading across the Virginia Northern \nNeck and Maryland Lower Eastern Shore Thursday night into early \nFriday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: An unsettled pattern develops late Thursday into\nthe weekend, with daily chances for showers or thunderstorms.\n\nThe dampening mid to upper trough will push the slow-moving \nfront across the region late tomorrow night into early Friday \nmorning. The boundary likely becomes stalled near or just south\nof the Albemarle Sound by midday Friday. A few showers or \nstorms remain possible along the boundary over southern \nlocations Friday into Friday evening.\n\nBetter areawide storm chances arrive Saturday as the stalled \nfront lifts back north and a few weak perturbations push across\nthe region. Considerable cloud cover and scattered to numerous\nshowers and storms, including the potential for some locally \nheavy downpours, will cap Saturday's highs in the low to mid 80s\nfor most, with upper 80s limited to the southern third of the \nCWA. There remains an area wide ERO that could suggest some\npotential hydro issues in the typical spots for Saturday, given\nthe moist, modestly unstable airmass and relatively slow steering\nflow.\n\nThe front washes out late weekend into early next week. Some\nadditional showers and storms remain possible Sunday, though \ncoverage will be noticeably lighter than Saturday. Looking \nahead, PoPs return back closer to climo normal Sun-Mon, trending\ndrier through the middle of next week. Meanwhile, the rebuilding\nSE ridge/rising heights look to portend a period of near to \nabove normal temperatures for the first half of next week.",
